    id: 29,
    title:
      "With the donation of a solar plant, Sicoob Credialto supports community healthcare services.",
    markdownTitle:
      "# With the donation of a solar plant, Sicoob Credialto supports community healthcare services.",
    context:
      "Sicoob Credialto was founded in 1991 by 28 rural producers from the Minas Gerais town of Piumhi, and since then it has built a relationship of development with the local community. True to this purpose, the cooperative identified a critical challenge that affected the entire region: the financial sustainability of Santa Casa de Piumhi.",
    challenge:
      "Before the project was implemented, Santa Casa de Piumhi, a reference hospital for seven municipalities in the interior of Minas Gerais, faced a challenging financial scenario. The institution incurred a monthly cost of approximately R$50,000 in electricity, an amount that compromised essential resources that could have been used to improve infrastructure and expand health services. This dependence on conventional energy sources limited the hospital's investment capacity and resulted in a high carbon footprint. In the community, awareness of the benefits of renewable energy was low, and distrust of donations made it difficult to modernize equipment and expand services.",
    development:
      "The strategy to make the project viable was executed through a clear and participatory action plan, divided into five main phases.\n* Awareness: the first stage focused on engaging the community and stakeholders through lectures, meetings, and educational activities to strengthen local support.\n* Planning: Strategic planning was then carried out, with technical and financial feasibility studies to guarantee the efficiency of the project.\n* Resource mobilization: the third phase was dedicated to raising R$181,000 through member campaigns and contributions from the Technical, Educational and Social Assistance Fund (FATES). One of the highlight initiatives was the cooperative’s donation of R$0.30 for each bill paid through the app.\n* Execution: With the funds secured and the support of Piumhi City Hall, the fourth stage consisted of contracting suppliers and installing the photovoltaic plant.\n* Monitoring: The final phase, in turn, involves the continuous monitoring of energy performance and the correct application of the resources saved by Santa Casa.",
    cooperName:
      "Alto São Francisco Open Admission Credit Union (Sicoob Credialto)",
    excerpt:
      "Sicoob Credialto led the construction of a photovoltaic plant for Santa Casa de Piumhi, the main reference hospital in its region. The initiative, funded through member campaigns and resources from FATES (Technical, Educational and Social Assistance Fund), reduced the institution’s energy costs by 40%, allowing the savings to be reinvested in improving healthcare infrastructure. The project combines energy sustainability, climate action and the strengthening of health services for a population of more than 100,000 people.",
    categories: ["Solar Energy", "Health", "Sustainability", "Community"],
    branch: "Credit",
    mainOds: 3,
    asideOds: [7, 11, 13, 17],
    state: "MG",
    objectives:
      'In this context, the "Sharing Energy, Multiplying Health" project was born out of the need to find a sustainable solution to Santa Casa\'s high energy costs. The main objective was to promote energy sustainability at the hospital by building a photovoltaic plant, with the aim of reducing electricity costs by at least 40%. In addition to financial savings, the initiative sought to generate a virtuous cycle: reinvesting the saved resources in improving regional healthcare, raising awareness about renewable energy, and strengthening the cooperative model as an agent of social and environmental transformation. The project was designed to be an innovative and replicable solution, with positive economic, environmental and social impacts.',
    result:
      "The photovoltaic plant in operation has generated a positive cycle of economic, social and environmental gains for the entire region. With the initiative, Santa Casa has achieved its goal of reducing its electricity costs by 40%, which represents savings of around R$12,000 per month. These resources, previously intended to cover operating expenses, are now being reinvested directly in improving the hospital's infrastructure and in new projects, such as modernizing the emergency generator and planning for the construction of a future hemodialysis unit. In the environmental pillar, the project contributed to reducing the institution's CO₂ emissions by approximately 25%, reinforcing the local commitment to mitigating climate change. In addition, the mobilization around the project strengthened community bonds and, through videos and events, raised public awareness about the benefits of clean energy, consolidating the image of the cooperative model as a true driver of sustainable development.",
